00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Chapter 2. Word Linking and Phrasing. Run your words together. As mentioned in the

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:12,520
previous chapter, in American English, words are not pronounced one by one.

3
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:17,260
Usually, the end-of-one word attaches to the beginning of the next word. This is

4
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:21,360
also true for initials, numbers, and spelling. Part of the glue that connects

5
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:26,540
sentences is an underlying hum or drone that only breaks when you come to a period

6
00:00:26,540 --> 00:00:31,040
and sometimes not even then. You have this underlying hum in your own language, and

7
00:00:31,040 --> 00:00:35,240
it helps a great deal toward making you sound like a native speaker. Once you have

8
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:39,500
a strong intonation, you need to connect all those stair steps together so that

9
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:44,140
each sentence sounds like one long word. This chapter is going to introduce you to

10
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:48,960
the idea of liaisons, the connections between words, which allow us to speak in

11
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,800
sound groups rather than in individual words. Just as we went over where to put

12
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:59,180
intonation, here you're going to learn how to connect words. Once you learn to use this

13
00:00:59,180 --> 00:01:03,520
technique, you can make the important leap from this practice book to other materials

14
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:08,140
in your own conversation. To make it easier for you to read, liaisons are

15
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:13,620
written like this. They tell me that I'm easier to understand. It could also be written, they

16
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,760
tell me that I'm easier to understand, but it would be too hard to read.
